model {
  basalt.poolRuntime -[sync]-> basalt.modelGateway "private Home peer byte transport"
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "Private Home peer transport carries opaque end-to-end authenticated bytes. Each direction has at most 64 KiB of outstanding credit and 16 KiB WebSocket messages. Credit returns only after the destination TCP write completes; protocol violations and unknown disconnects close the peer without replay. Independent half closes preserve a reply after the sender finishes. This transport alone does not establish runtime ownership, admission or a safe legacy transition."
    }
  }
  extend basalt.poolRuntime {
    metadata {
      constraint "Fleet Home identity is a retained ordinal from 1 through 128, included in the encrypted startup capsule and its applied-settings fingerprint. Each ordinal advertises its own private loopback port while Home listens on 8329. The local node keeps a fresh boot-specific certificate and reaches its own Home locally; peer connections use bounded private transport. Each Home retains six frontend PostgreSQL connections and a distinct application name. A missing ordinal preserves the legacy address, name and disabled discovery. Fleet deployment and legacy coexistence require separately admitted routing and migration proofs. The serving endpoint durably counts each admitted model response through its HTTP body or WebSocket lifetime. Draining fences new requests before proving the exact boot has zero retained activity. Controller memory never establishes drain. Legacy requests count durably before warming or dispatch. An untracked old boot additionally requires zero connections on its model listener from both operating system TCP tables, after durable selection excludes it and before a second matching boot observation. The closed read-only diagnostic emits only a count, bounds its table reads and stops after two seconds. Failed observations stay unknown. Destruction has its own durable intent; a lost response never repeats it. A signed operator command can add the fixed bundled peer helper to an admitted retained boot without replacing it. Its source digest and configuration bind a durable intent before exec. Readback verifies the namespace process start time, exact command bytes and original nonce, separately retaining the platform process id. An uncertain exec or lost durable reply permits inspection only, including after controller reconstruction; it never starts a second helper."
    }
  }
  extend basalt.build {
    metadata {
      constraint "Python networking analysis resolves TCP connection hosts through literal address tuples and ordinary helper propagation. Loopback listeners use the atomic create-and-bind factory; raw IP socket constructors, dynamic destinations and non-loopback listeners stay rejected. Unix-domain socket pairs are local only when their family is explicit. Private Home peer hosts must appear in the architecture with their caller relationship. The Worker bundles its legacy peer program from the canonical Python modules; the production bundler rejects stale generated bytes. Operator input cannot supply a program."
    }
  }
}
